{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact S1 head ab4367f9a8ce575f3b15f5dd4cb238309ad8edf8 on builder/serving-windows-s1-20261111, all 14 artifacts, for request 8bd2c9d2 under promise 75b128e9 and review request 9a6686ce. Manifest SHA-256 814d901685b2f40610e6406f1d0134cce39098eaa81c1e177a05b254c09416e4. The head closes findings 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4 and 1.6 as 8bd2c9d2 asks. It adds no contract refusal or reason: serving exhaustion uses the existing `unavailable` answer with `rate-limited`.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- 1.1, `ServingLimits.account` (`packages/scope/src/serving-limits.ts`). The address is charged before the key. An address past its limit returns false before the key gets a window, so one address can open at most 30 creation key windows an hour. Creation, `found` and signed reads each keep their own map. A full map sends a new identity to one overflow window for that operation, capped at the lower of the key and address limits. A request charges that window once, even when both identities land in it. No live window is evicted.\n- 1.4, the reservation. HTTP creation calls `admitCreationAddress` before `body(request)`. That call charges the address alone through the fixed `ScopeIndex` owner. The later key charge passes a null address, so the address is charged once. The overflow reservation carries the owner's incarnation and the window's incarnation. It skips the key charge only for that same live overflow window. `scopedApi` is module-private, so no body or public `api()` argument can supply a reservation. `has` stays a fresh SQL read.\n- 1.2, `siteReadAddressKey` (`site/window.ts`). It now calls `addressKey`, the same function that `limits.ts` re-exports from `packages/bytes/src/address.ts`, so the addresses of one IPv6 /64 share one key. Non-IP labels get an `opaque:` key and cannot alias an `address:` key. A full table counts a new visitor in one 240-read overflow window.\n- 1.3, the sandbox (`apps/sandbox/worker.ts`). It no longer forwards `cf-connecting-ip` as a header. It passes the edge address as the third argument of `native` and `nativeStream`, and the room hands it to `site` and `route`. The `screen` path still carries no address. The comment is corrected. The note explains why the gateway adds no allowlist of scope IDs: the descriptor does not name every scope that the application calls.\n- 1.6, `found`. `submit` sends a `found` intent through the fixed owner's `found` table before it addresses the target. `admit` refuses a `found` request whose intent kind is not `found`.\n- The entry-point inventory now calls `submit` `self-signed`, because an open-register `found` takes a valid signature without a membership grant. `scripts/security-entry-points.mjs` and `docs/security/entry-points.md` agree.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main d4cfae05 is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. The builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 14 paths that the 14 artifacts cover, and the primary artifact is neither stale nor retired. Apart from `notes/`, bfc67122 equals acf797a7. From bfc67122 to the head, only the note and its manifest change.\n- Cheap checks. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings. The commit-message check passes: 9 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean.\n- Tests, on my own extracted copy, with no shared validation slot. `node --test scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` passes 12 of 12, and `node scripts/security-entry-points.mjs --check` exits 0. I did not run the four scope test files. They run in workerd, and the gate and the two control units cover them.\n- Evidence. The committed manifest hashes to `814d9016…16e4`, as the request says. Its 14 files match their SHA-256 values in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-12-s1-final-curated-provenance`. The gate counts agree with END 66e3dc61: 5,903 obligations, with 5,900 passes and three planned skips.\n- Controls. I accept both. Control A reverses the address and key order, and `serving-limits.test.ts:31` sees 8,192 key rows instead of 31. Control B keeps raw IP spellings, and `site-window.test.ts:26` sees 4,096 admissions instead of 240. Control C drops the gateway address, and the second visitor gets 429 instead of 404 at `serving-admission.test.ts:246`. Control D moves the creation refusal after the body read, and line 281 sees one body read and 400. Control E bypasses the `found` refusal, and line 260 sees `unavailable` instead of `rate-limited`. Each mutant child exited 1 at its named assertion.\n- CI, under ruling b5283835. GitHub reports Page run 43201044890 and Card run 43201044687 as push events, attempt 1, completed and successful, at bfc67122. Manual Page run 43201184843 at the same commit failed. The Page sources and `scripts/demo-captures.ts` at bfc67122 equal main d4cfae05, so S1 did not cause that failure. F-2 (0f327b6b) owns it. The head itself has no CI run, because only the note and the manifest changed. I accept bfc67122's push runs as S1's CI, as b5283835 rules.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Serving accounting stays in the one fixed owner and stays out of native judgment. The sandbox passes the edge address as an explicit argument and never as a header. Each operation owns its table and its overflow window.\n\nSecurity: no finding against 8bd2c9d2. Each of the three traces now leaves room for a new caller, and the controls show that the tests catch each regression. One limit remains, and the documents do not state it. The creation table holds 8,192 windows for an hour, and one address key opens at most 31 of them: its own window and 30 key windows. About 265 distinct IPv4 addresses or IPv6 /64s therefore fill the creation table. After that, every new identity shares 10 creations an hour until rows expire. One IPv6 /48 holds 65,536 /64s. The overflow window bounds the cost, as 8bd2c9d2 asks, but a sender with a /48 can still hold creation for newcomers for an hour. Callers that already have windows keep them. State that number in section 7 of `docs/security/entry-points.md` in a later head. If it matters before the live window, a coarser key for the overflow decision is the smallest change.\n\nSimplification: no required change. Two notes for a later head:\n\n1. `submit` applies the `found` window only when `boundedServingIntent` succeeds. A `found` intent over 1 MiB, or one that fails the strict parse, goes to the target with no `found` charge. The target then refuses it, at the cost of an ordinary submit. Charging the address for that case too would make the rule simpler to state.\n2. `account` takes five parameters, and the reservation applies only to creation. A separate creation method that takes the reservation would let the shared method keep four.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing and deploys the Workers afterward, as 8bd2c9d2 says.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:961caefaf88da4171cb89279d54921550cea0d74","binding":"assigned","head":"ab4367f9a8ce575f3b15f5dd4cb238309ad8edf8","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ea78159e974b8ec45d4e9be3497b2fc62fdbe60c\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:75b128e9f86b452f70c156e9f5c00b1d5545c069\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a66bc575a6c2d6a2f332987714fe3c08984a7ec5","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}